{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793746009,"nonce":"tLDyKt0MSKZmV8WfICu0Cq6zzKE=","message":"Landed in commit d11cc252a6409cebaade896304f62abe5f86a795.\n\nImplemented bounded reuse of register files, union slots, OSR bookkeeping and bytecode call argument buffers. Active frames own separate storage; successful, error and OSR exits unregister precise roots before recycling. Owned values are cleared. Idle buffers are capped at 1 MiB and 32 frames per interpreter. The public Vec-taking call APIs and ownership-taking host dispatchers retain their existing contracts; an indirect host dispatcher takes its argument buffer rather than recycling it.\n\nNUC allocation-audit build, after bytecode/frame warmup, per fib(12) invocation containing 465 interpreted frames:\n- Roots disabled: 931 -\u003e 2 Rust allocations; requested bytes 334803 -\u003e 75.\n- Roots enabled: 1400 -\u003e 471 allocations; requested bytes 350995 -\u003e 16267.\n- No reallocations in either warmed arm. Counts exclude direct libc/slab/native-library allocations. Pooled retained capacity is not a claim of lower RSS. Allocation-audit timing is excluded from the speed verdict.\n\nOrdinary release NUC binaries, 15 alternating rounds, CPU affinity 0,2,4,6:\n- Recursive frame, roots off: 158.57 -\u003e 157.62 ns (essentially unchanged).\n- Recursive frame, roots on: 204.58 -\u003e 208.46 ns (1.9% slower).\n- One-symbol frame: 102.72 -\u003e 105.06 ns without roots; 150.29 -\u003e 150.06 ns with roots.\n- 24-site symbol frame: 210.54 -\u003e 189.56 ns without roots (1.11x); 257.51 -\u003e 223.91 ns with roots (1.15x).\n- Cold symbol frame: 9130.93 -\u003e 8825.48 ns.\n- Scalar-loop control: 41.09 -\u003e 42.21 ns/iteration.\n\nNormal LLVM-enabled frontend comparison: seven alternating rounds, all 17 Lua benchmarks and float/fannkuch/nbody_modified Python kernels at five iterations. Every reference checksum/output agrees. Geometric